Subject: [PATCH] fonts: match suffixed family names the way the platform does

On Windows, Chromium resolves a name like "Arial Narrow" by stripping a
known weight or stretch suffix and matching the base family. The
profile's font allowlist follows the same rule: a name passes if it is
listed, or if one stripped suffix leaves a listed family. Linux strips
only the stretch suffixes it emulates for a Windows profile, and macOS
matches names as written. Without this, a profile listing Arial hides
Arial Narrow, which every real Windows machine with Arial reports. One
FontCache function serves text rendering, document.fonts.check() and CSS
local(), so they agree.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.h
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.h
@@ -130,6 +130,21 @@
       const FontDescription&,
       const AtomicString& unique_font_name);
 
+  // Whether the profile's font enumeration allowlist hides `family` from the
+  // families a page is allowed to observe.
+  //
+  // The single predicate every page-visible enumeration route consults, so
+  // that they cannot disagree: a filter applied to document.fonts.check() but
+  // not to canvas measurement would have the two contradict each other, which
+  // is a signal no real machine produces. Lives here because the suffix rules
+  // it has to mirror are the platform font caches' own.
+  //
+  // Subtractive only. A true answer declines to look a family up; it never
+  // makes an absent family resolve, because the platform matcher still has to
+  // find a face afterwards. Generic families are the caller's business and are
+  // not recognised here.
+  static bool ProfileHidesFamily(const AtomicString& family);
+
   static String FirstAvailableOrFirst(const String&);
 
   void AddClient(FontCacheClient*);
